            ('            "in": {"au": {"value": 0.0001, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 20000.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 6e-10, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 3.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.03, "figures": 1, "prints": null}},\n            "figures": 1,\n            "source": "Meredith et al. (2014), doi:10.1002/2014JA020064, and Li, Tu, Selesnick and Huang (2024), J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 129:e2023JA032171, doi:10.1029/2023JA032171 -- both state the outer belt extending from 3 Earth radii, in their introductions. Open full text at nora.nerc.ac.uk and par.nsf.gov.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_BELT_INNER_EDGE",\n            "note": "The layer below is Paulikas and Blake (1979) with Baker et al. (1986) for Meredith, and Ganushkina et al. (2011) with Van Allen et al. (1958) for Li. Baker et al. (2018) sec. 2 gives the same inner edge from SAMPEX and reports it reaching L ~ 2.5 under strong driving."\n',
             '            "in": {"au": {"value": 0.0001, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 20000.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 6e-10, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 3.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.03, "figures": 1, "prints": null}},\n            "figures": 1,\n            "source": "Meredith et al. (2014), doi:10.1002/2014JA020064 -- introduction, first paragraph: the outer belt extends from 3 to 7 R_E. Li, Tu, Selesnick and Huang (2024), \\"Modeling the contribution of precipitation loss to a radiation belt electron dropout observed by Van Allen Probes\\", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 129, e2023JA032171, doi:10.1029/2023JA032171 -- introduction, first paragraph, the same span independently.",\n            "source_note": "both state the outer belt extending from 3 Earth radii, in their introductions. Open full text at nora.nerc.ac.uk and par.nsf.gov.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_BELT_INNER_EDGE",\n            "note": "The layer below is Paulikas and Blake (1979) with Baker et al. (1986) for Meredith, and Ganushkina et al. (2011) with Van Allen et al. (1958) for Li. Baker et al. (2018) sec. 2 gives the same inner edge from SAMPEX and reports it reaching L ~ 2.5 under strong driving."\n'),
            ('            "prints": 2,\n            "figures": "exact",\n            "source": "Li et al. (2025), J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics, doi:10.1029/2024JA033504 -- sec. 1, the outer belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5; the value served is our midpoint of that band. Kellerman et al. (2014), as reported in arXiv:1809.00902 -- maximum electron flux at L = 4-5; the arXiv paper is the document opened and Kellerman is the layer below it.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_RADII",\n            "note": "A flux PEAK, not an edge. The unit is L, the McIlwain parameter, which equals geocentric distance in Earth radii only where a field line crosses the magnetic equator -- so any string printing this value says \'at the equator\'. Baker et al. (2018) was removed from this citation on 2026-09-14: his figure 30 uses L* = 4.5 as a selected analysis location rather than a universal peak, and his span figure belongs on the edge row below."\n          },\n          "outer_belt_band_low": {"value": 4.0, "unit": "l_shell", "figures": 1, "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_BAND_LOW_L"},\n          "outer_belt_band_high": {"value": 5.0, "unit": "l_shell", "figures": 1, "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_BAND_HIGH_L"},\n          "outer_belt_inner_edge": {\n            "value": 3.0,\n',
             '            "prints": 2,\n            "figures": "exact",\n            "source": "Li, X., Xiang, Z., Mei, Y., O\'Brien, D., Brennan, D., Zhao, H., Baker, D. N. and Temerin, M. A. (2025), \\"A New Electron and Proton Radiation Belt Identified by CIRBE/REPTile-2 Measurements After the Magnetic Super Storm of 10 May 2024\\", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 130, e2024JA033504, doi:10.1029/2024JA033504 -- sec. 1: the outer radiation belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5. This row is the lower end of that band. Li et al. (2015), doi:10.1002/2014JA020777, sec. 1 -- greatest intensity between 4 and 5 equatorial R_E for electrons above 500 keV. Kellerman et al. (2014), as reported in \\"Electron intensity measurements by the Cluster/RAPID/IES instrument in Earth\'s radiation belts and ring current\\" (2018, arXiv:1809.00902) -- maximum electron flux at L = 4-5. The arXiv paper is the document opened; Kellerman is the layer below it. These two corroborate; they were carried on the peak row\'s \\"# Declared:\\" line until 2026-09-22 and were not re-read here.; Li et al. (2025), doi:10.1029/2024JA033504 -- sec. 1: the outer radiation belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5. This row is the upper end of that band. Corroborated as the row above.",\n            "source_note": "sec. 1, the outer belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5; the value served is our midpoint of that band. Kellerman et al. (2014), as reported in arXiv:1809.00902 -- maximum electron flux at L = 4-5; the arXiv paper is the document opened and Kellerman is the layer below it.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_RADII",\n            "note": "A flux PEAK, not an edge. The unit is L, the McIlwain parameter, which equals geocentric distance in Earth radii only where a field line crosses the magnetic equator -- so any string printing this value says \'at the equator\'. Baker et al. (2018) was removed from this citation on 2026-09-14: his figure 30 uses L* = 4.5 as a selected analysis location rather than a universal peak, and his span figure belongs on the edge row below."\n          },\n          "outer_belt_band_low": {"value": 4.0, "unit": "l_shell", "figures": 1, "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_BAND_LOW_L", "source": "Li, X., Xiang, Z., Mei, Y., O\'Brien, D., Brennan, D., Zhao, H., Baker, D. N. and Temerin, M. A. (2025), \\"A New Electron and Proton Radiation Belt Identified by CIRBE/REPTile-2 Measurements After the Magnetic Super Storm of 10 May 2024\\", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 130, e2024JA033504, doi:10.1029/2024JA033504 -- sec. 1: the outer radiation belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5. This row is the lower end of that band. Li et al. (2015), doi:10.1002/2014JA020777, sec. 1 -- greatest intensity between 4 and 5 equatorial R_E for electrons above 500 keV. Kellerman et al. (2014), as reported in \\"Electron intensity measurements by the Cluster/RAPID/IES instrument in Earth\'s radiation belts and ring current\\" (2018, arXiv:1809.00902) -- maximum electron flux at L = 4-5. The arXiv paper is the document opened; Kellerman is the layer below it. These two corroborate; they were carried on the peak row\'s \\"# Declared:\\" line until 2026-09-22 and were not re-read here."},\n          "outer_belt_band_high": {"value": 5.0, "unit": "l_shell", "figures": 1, "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_BAND_HIGH_L", "source": "Li et al. (2025), doi:10.1029/2024JA033504 -- sec. 1: the outer radiation belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5. This row is the upper end of that band. Corroborated as the row above."},\n          "outer_belt_inner_edge": {\n            "value": 3.0,\n'),
            ('            "in": {"au": {"value": 5e-05, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 7000.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 2.3e-10, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 1.1, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.01, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},\n            "figures": 2,\n            "source": "Meredith, Horne, Kersten, Fraser and Grew (2014), J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 119:5328, doi:10.1002/2014JA020064 -- introduction, first paragraph: the inner belt extends from about 1.1 to 2 Earth radii in the geomagnetic equatorial plane. Open full text at nora.nerc.ac.uk. Meredith states this as background and passes it on from Baker et al. (2007), which is the layer below rather than the source.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_INNER_BELT_INNER_EDGE",\n            "note": "Koskinen and Kilpua (2022) sec. 1.1 corroborate 1.1 to 2 R_E but are NOT the source, because they state it for the inner ELECTRON belt and put the energetic protons over about 1.1 to 3 R_E. The figure matched and the scope did not."\n',
             '            "in": {"au": {"value": 5e-05, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 7000.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 2.3e-10, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 1.1, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.01, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},\n            "figures": 2,\n            "source": "Meredith, N. P., Horne, R. B., Kersten, T., Fraser, B. J. and Grew, R. S. (2014), \\"Global morphology and spectral properties of EMIC waves derived from CRRES observations\\", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 119, 5328-5342, doi:10.1002/2014JA020064 -- introduction, first paragraph: the inner belt extends from about 1.1 to 2 Earth radii in the geomagnetic equatorial plane.",\n            "source_note": "introduction, first paragraph: the inner belt extends from about 1.1 to 2 Earth radii in the geomagnetic equatorial plane. Open full text at nora.nerc.ac.uk. Meredith states this as background and passes it on from Baker et al. (2007), which is the layer below rather than the source.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_INNER_BELT_INNER_EDGE",\n            "note": "Koskinen and Kilpua (2022) sec. 1.1 corroborate 1.1 to 2 R_E but are NOT the source, because they state it for the inner ELECTRON belt and put the energetic protons over about 1.1 to 3 R_E. The figure matched and the scope did not."\n'),
            ('            "in": {"au": {"value": 6e-05, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 9600.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 3.1e-10, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 1.5, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.014, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},\n            "figures": 2,\n            "source": "Baker et al. (2018), Space Sci. Rev. 214:17, doi:10.1007/s11214-017-0452-7 -- sec. 2, inner-zone proton fluxes peak near geocentric r ~ 1.5 R_E",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_INNER_RADII",\n            "note": "A flux PEAK, not an edge. The edges are their own rows below, as of 2026-09-14 (L-305 item 7); this note carried the span in prose until then, which is what let the served figure and the orrery\'s disagree."\n',
             '            "in": {"au": {"value": 6e-05, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 9600.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 3.1e-10, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 1.5, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.014, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},\n            "figures": 2,\n            "source": "Baker, D. N. et al. (2018), \\"Space Weather Effects in the Earth\'s Radiation Belts\\", Space Sci. Rev. 214:17, doi:10.1007/s11214-017-0452-7 -- sec. 2: inner-zone proton fluxes peak near geocentric r ~ 1.5 R_E. Geocentric, equatorial, which is the frame this row is in.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_INNER_RADII",\n            "note": "A flux PEAK, not an edge. The edges are their own rows below, as of 2026-09-14 (L-305 item 7); this note carried the span in prose until then, which is what let the served figure and the orrery\'s disagree."\n'),
            ('            "unit": "deg",\n            "figures": 5,\n            "source": "Alken et al. (2021), International Geomagnetic Reference Field: the thirteenth generation, Earth Planets Space 73:49, doi:10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x -- the angle between the geomagnetic dipole axis and Earth\'s rotation axis at epoch 2020.0. The paper prints no tilt of its own: constants_new.py computes it from the paper\'s three degree-1 Gauss coefficients, the relation the paper gives for the geomagnetic poles, and serves it at five figures. It is decreasing by about 0.049 degrees a year (magnetic_tilt_rate, from the same coefficients\' published rates), so any quotation of it carries its epoch. The belts\' hover says the magnetic equator turns with Earth: the paper\'s Table 4 places the 2020.0 north geomagnetic pole at a point on Earth, 80.65 N, -72.68 E (constants_new.py, read 2026-09-21), so the dipole turns with the ground beneath it. This sentence said \'rounded to a tenth of a degree\' and \'about 0.05 deg per decade\' until 2026-09-26; the decade figure was ten times too small (L-322 Stage D, gallery patch 3).",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_DIPOLE_TILT_DEG",\n            "note": "Served so the hover can quote it. The rings are NOT tilted by it. They are drawn in the equatorial plane, which is the daily average of the magnetic one, because a tilted plane needs a direction as well as an angle and that direction turns once a day while this scene is frozen (L-231, Tony\'s ruling 2026-09-15)."\n          },\n          "magnetic_tilt_rate": {"value": -0.0493, "unit": "deg_per_year", "figures": 3, "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_DIPOLE_TILT_RATE_DEG_PER_YEAR"},\n          "_frame": "The edge rows below are geocentric distances in the geomagnetic equatorial plane, which is the frame their sources state. Tony\'s ruling, 2026-09-14 (L-323 ruling B, second amendment). The literature is mixed -- Y. X. Li et al. (2023) states the outer span in L shells and Baker et al. (2018) states it both ways -- so each row names the paper its figure follows and the frame travels with the row rather than with the block.",\n          "inner_belt_distance": {\n',
             '            "unit": "deg",\n            "figures": 5,\n            "source": "Alken et al. (2021), \\"International Geomagnetic Reference Field: the thirteenth generation\\", Earth Planets Space 73:49, doi:10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x -- for the RELATION: the geomagnetic poles are computed from the three degree-1 Gauss coefficients, which is what this row does. The paper prints no tilt of its own. Its Table 4 gives the 2020.0 north geomagnetic pole at 80.65 N, -72.68 E; these coefficients reproduce it as 80.6512 N, -72.6797 E (read 2026-09-21 by Claude Opus 5).",\n            "source_note": "the angle between the geomagnetic dipole axis and Earth\'s rotation axis at epoch 2020.0. The paper prints no tilt of its own: constants_new.py computes it from the paper\'s three degree-1 Gauss coefficients, the relation the paper gives for the geomagnetic poles, and serves it at five figures. It is decreasing by about 0.049 degrees a year (magnetic_tilt_rate, from the same coefficients\' published rates), so any quotation of it carries its epoch. The belts\' hover says the magnetic equator turns with Earth: the paper\'s Table 4 places the 2020.0 north geomagnetic pole at a point on Earth, 80.65 N, -72.68 E (constants_new.py, read 2026-09-21), so the dipole turns with the ground beneath it. This sentence said \'rounded to a tenth of a degree\' and \'about 0.05 deg per decade\' until 2026-09-26; the decade figure was ten times too small (L-322 Stage D, gallery patch 3).",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_DIPOLE_TILT_DEG",\n            "note": "Served so the hover can quote it. The rings are NOT tilted by it. They are drawn in the equatorial plane, which is the daily average of the magnetic one, because a tilted plane needs a direction as well as an angle and that direction turns once a day while this scene is frozen (L-231, Tony\'s ruling 2026-09-15)."\n          },\n          "magnetic_tilt_rate": {"value": -0.0493, "unit": "deg_per_year", "figures": 3, "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_DIPOLE_TILT_RATE_DEG_PER_YEAR", "source": "IGRF-13 coefficient file igrf13coeffs.txt -- the predicted rate of change of g(1,0) over 2020-2025 is 5.7 nT per year. Alken et al. (2021), doi:10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x.; IGRF-13 coefficient file igrf13coeffs.txt (IAGA Working Group V-MOD, served by NOAA NCEI) -- g(1,0) at epoch 2020.0 is -29404.8 nT. The model is Alken, P. et al. (2021), \\"International Geomagnetic Reference Field: the thirteenth generation\\", Earth Planets Space 73:49, doi:10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x, which names this file as the digital form of its Table 2.; IGRF-13 coefficient file igrf13coeffs.txt -- g(1,1) at epoch 2020.0 is -1450.9 nT. Alken et al. (2021), doi:10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x.; IGRF-13 coefficient file igrf13coeffs.txt -- h(1,1) at epoch 2020.0 is 4652.5 nT. Alken et al. (2021), doi:10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x.; IGRF-13 coefficient file igrf13coeffs.txt -- the predicted rate of change of g(1,1) over 2020-2025 is 7.4 nT per year. Alken et al. (2021), doi:10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x.; IGRF-13 coefficient file igrf13coeffs.txt -- the predicted rate of change of h(1,1) over 2020-2025 is -25.9 nT per year. Alken et al. (2021), doi:10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x."},\n          "_frame": "The edge rows below are geocentric distances in the geomagnetic equatorial plane, which is the frame their sources state. Tony\'s ruling, 2026-09-14 (L-323 ruling B, second amendment). The literature is mixed -- Y. X. Li et al. (2023) states the outer span in L shells and Baker et al. (2018) states it both ways -- so each row names the paper its figure follows and the frame travels with the row rather than with the block.",\n          "inner_belt_distance": {\n'),
            ('            "unit": "km",\n            "in": {"au": {"value": 4.263521e-05, "figures": 8, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 6378.1366, "figures": 8, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 2.0670133e-10, "figures": 8, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 1.0, "figures": "exact", "prints": 1}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.0091679411, "figures": 8, "prints": null}},\n            "uncertainty": "0.0001",\n            "figures": 8,\n            "source": "IERS Conventions (2010), Petit & Luzum (eds.), IERS Technical Note No. 36, Table 1.1",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_EQUATORIAL_RADIUS_KM"\n          }\n        },\n        "van_allen_belts": {\n',
             '            "unit": "km",\n            "in": {"au": {"value": 4.263521e-05, "figures": 8, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 6378.1366, "figures": 8, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 2.0670133e-10, "figures": 8, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 1.0, "figures": "exact", "prints": 1}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.0091679411, "figures": 8, "prints": null}},\n            "uncertainty": "0.0001",\n            "figures": 8,\n            "source": "IERS Conventions (2010), Petit & Luzum (eds.), IERS Technical Note No. 36, Table 1.1; IAU B3 rounds to 6378.1 km",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_EQUATORIAL_RADIUS_KM"\n          }\n        },\n        "van_allen_belts": {\n'),
            ('              "in": {"au": {"value": 0.009, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 1400000.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 4.5e-08, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 220.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 2.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},\n              "figures": 2,\n              "source": "Slavin, Tsurutani, Smith, Jones and Sibeck (1983), \'Average configuration of the distant (less than 220-earth-radii) magnetotail - Initial ISEE-3 magnetic field results\', Geophys. Res. Lett. 10(10):973, doi:10.1029/GL010i010p00973 -- the abstract states that the magnetotail retains much of its near-Earth structure out to X = -220 Earth radii. Abstract open at ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19830066648. The same abstract\'s figures for where flaring ends and for the tail\'s diameter are superseded by the same mission\'s 1985 averages, served here as flare_end and diameter.",\n              "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_MAGNETOTAIL_OBSERVED_RADII",\n              "note": "Read as OBSERVED TO AT LEAST, not as an edge. 220 R_E is ISEE-3\'s reach on its first two tail passes, so the figure is bounded by the orbit rather than by where the tail ends. Two further signatures are more distant and less like a tail: Ness, Scearce and Cantarano (1967), doi:10.1029/JZ072i015p03769, report Pioneer 7 in the expected tail region at 900 to 1,050 R_E over a week in 1966, intermittently and with no coherent tail, the title saying \'probable\'; and Intriligator et al. (1979), Geophys. Res. Lett. 6:585, report tail-ASSOCIATED phenomena near 3,100 R_E, read by later work as signatures disconnected from Earth."\n',
             '              "in": {"au": {"value": 0.009, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 1400000.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 4.5e-08, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 220.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 2.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},\n              "figures": 2,\n              "source": "Slavin, J. A., Tsurutani, B. T., Smith, E. J., Jones, D. E. and Sibeck, D. G. (1983), \\"Average configuration of the distant (less than 220-earth-radii) magnetotail - Initial ISEE-3 magnetic field results\\", Geophys. Res. Lett. 10(10), 973-976, doi:10.1029/GL010i010p00973 -- the abstract states that the magnetotail retains much of its near-Earth structure out to X = -220 Earth radii, that flaring ceases at 100-120 R_E, and that the tail diameter settles near 60 R_E.",\n              "source_note": "the abstract states that the magnetotail retains much of its near-Earth structure out to X = -220 Earth radii. Abstract open at ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19830066648. The same abstract\'s figures for where flaring ends and for the tail\'s diameter are superseded by the same mission\'s 1985 averages, served here as flare_end and diameter.",\n              "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_MAGNETOTAIL_OBSERVED_RADII",\n              "note": "Read as OBSERVED TO AT LEAST, not as an edge. 220 R_E is ISEE-3\'s reach on its first two tail passes, so the figure is bounded by the orbit rather than by where the tail ends. Two further signatures are more distant and less like a tail: Ness, Scearce and Cantarano (1967), doi:10.1029/JZ072i015p03769, report Pioneer 7 in the expected tail region at 900 to 1,050 R_E over a week in 1966, intermittently and with no coherent tail, the title saying \'probable\'; and Intriligator et al. (1979), Geophys. Res. Lett. 6:585, report tail-ASSOCIATED phenomena near 3,100 R_E, read by later work as signatures disconnected from Earth."\n'),
