            ('            "figures": 3\n            },\n            "scatter": {"value": 0.69, "unit": "r_earth", "in": {"au": {"value": 2.9e-05, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 4400.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 1.43e-10, "figures": 3, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 0.69, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.0063, "figures": 2, "prints": null}}, "figures": 2, "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_BOW_SHOCK_JELINEK_SCATTER_RADII"},\n            "info_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow_shocks_in_astrophysics",\n            "hover": "Jelinek et al. (2012), at dynamic pressure {pressure} nPa\\nDrawn to {cut_angle} deg from the nose, which is how far round the crossings the fit was made from actually reached.\\nThat is where the drawing stops, not where the shock ends.\\nNot tilted: the fit is symmetric about the Sun line.",\n            "color": "rgb(255, 200, 150)",\n            "opacity": 0.25,\n            "source": "Jelinek, Nemecek and Safrankova (2012), J. Geophys. Res. 117:A05208, doi:10.1029/2011JA017252 -- eq. 14, R_BS = 15.02 p^(-1/6.55), evaluated at the declared pressure of 2 nPa. The paper states no uncertainty on its fitted numbers; how far real crossings fall from the fit (fig. 7) is given in the hover. Corroboration: Lugaz et al. (2016), Nat. Commun. 7:13001, doi:10.1038/ncomms13001 -- 11-14 R_E subsolar under normal solar wind, which contains this.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_BOW_SHOCK_STANDOFF_RADII",\n            "note": "Quiet-time nominal, at the same declared solar wind pressure as the magnetopause. An earlier note here cited Farris & Russell (1994) for the model FORM; that was a miscitation -- it is a relation for the standoff at a given Mach number and takes obstacle shape as an input, not a shape model. Removed 2026-09-12 (L-305)."\n',
             '            "figures": 3\n            },\n            "scatter": {"value": 0.69, "unit": "r_earth", "in": {"au": {"value": 2.9e-05, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 4400.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 1.43e-10, "figures": 3, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 0.69, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.0063, "figures": 2, "prints": null}}, "figures": 2, "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_BOW_SHOCK_JELINEK_SCATTER_RADII", "source": "Jelinek, Nemecek and Safrankova (2012), J. Geophys. Res. 117, A05208, doi:10.1029/2011JA017252 -- fig. 7: the scatter of observed crossings about the bow shock model is 0.69 R_E. The same figure gives 0.76 R_E for their magnetopause."},\n            "info_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bow_shocks_in_astrophysics",\n            "hover": "Jelinek et al. (2012), at dynamic pressure {pressure} nPa\\nDrawn to {cut_angle} deg from the nose, which is how far round the crossings the fit was made from actually reached.\\nThat is where the drawing stops, not where the shock ends.\\nNot tilted: the fit is symmetric about the Sun line.",\n            "color": "rgb(255, 200, 150)",\n            "opacity": 0.25,\n            "source": "Jelinek, K., Nemecek, Z. and Safrankova, J. (2012), \\"A new approach to magnetopause and bow shock modeling based on automated region identification\\", J. Geophys. Res. 117, A05208, doi:10.1029/2011JA017252 -- eq. 14, p. 5: R_BS = 15.02 p^(-1/6.55), so 15.02 R_E is the bow shock stand-off at p = 1 nPa. Fitted from Themis crossings, in aberrated GSE.; Jelinek et al. (2012), doi:10.1029/2011JA017252 -- eq. 14, p. 5: the pressure exponent of R_BS = 15.02 p^(-1/6.55).",\n            "source_note": "eq. 14, R_BS = 15.02 p^(-1/6.55), evaluated at the declared pressure of 2 nPa. The paper states no uncertainty on its fitted numbers; how far real crossings fall from the fit (fig. 7) is given in the hover. Corroboration: Lugaz et al. (2016), Nat. Commun. 7:13001, doi:10.1038/ncomms13001 -- 11-14 R_E subsolar under normal solar wind, which contains this.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_BOW_SHOCK_STANDOFF_RADII",\n            "note": "Quiet-time nominal, at the same declared solar wind pressure as the magnetopause. An earlier note here cited Farris & Russell (1994) for the model FORM; that was a miscitation -- it is a relation for the standoff at a given Mach number and takes obstacle shape as an input, not a shape model. Removed 2026-09-12 (L-305)."\n'),
