            ('            "figures": 3\n            },\n            "scatter": {"value": 1.23, "unit": "r_earth", "in": {"au": {"value": 5.2e-05, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 7800.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 2.54e-10, "figures": 3, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 1.23, "figures": 3, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.0113, "figures": 3, "prints": null}}, "figures": 3, "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_MAGNETOPAUSE_SHUE_SCATTER_RADII"},\n            "info_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnetopause",\n            "hover": "Shue et al. (1998), for the solar wind assumed here:\\nBz {bz} nT, dynamic pressure {pressure} nPa\\nDrawn to {cut_angle} deg from the nose, as far as the paper plots its model. Beyond that angle the boundary is drawn as the magnetotail.\\nNot tilted: the model is symmetric about the Sun line.",\n            "color": "rgb(180, 180, 255)",\n            "opacity": 0.25,\n            "source": "Shue et al. (1998), J. Geophys. Res. 103:17691, doi:10.1029/98JA01103 -- eq. 10 evaluated at the declared conditions (Bz = 0 nT, Dp = 2 nPa). Corroboration: Lugaz et al. (2016), doi:10.1038/ncomms13001 -- typical subsolar magnetopause 9-11 R_E, which contains this.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_MAGNETOPAUSE_STANDOFF_RADII",\n            "note": "Quiet-time nominal. Under storm compression it can fall inside geostationary orbit (6.6 R_E)."\n',
             '            "figures": 3\n            },\n            "scatter": {"value": 1.23, "unit": "r_earth", "in": {"au": {"value": 5.2e-05, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 7800.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 2.54e-10, "figures": 3, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 1.23, "figures": 3, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.0113, "figures": 3, "prints": null}}, "figures": 3, "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_MAGNETOPAUSE_SHUE_SCATTER_RADII", "source": "Shue et al. (1998), doi:10.1029/98JA01103, p. 17,697 -- the improved model\'s standard deviation against the observed magnetopause crossings it was fitted to is 1.23 R_E."},\n            "info_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnetopause",\n            "hover": "Shue et al. (1998), for the solar wind assumed here:\\nBz {bz} nT, dynamic pressure {pressure} nPa\\nDrawn to {cut_angle} deg from the nose, as far as the paper plots its model. Beyond that angle the boundary is drawn as the magnetotail.\\nNot tilted: the model is symmetric about the Sun line.",\n            "color": "rgb(180, 180, 255)",\n            "opacity": 0.25,\n            "source": "Shue, J.-H., Song, P., Russell, C. T., Steinberg, J. T., Chao, J. K., Zastenker, G., Vaisberg, O. L., Kokubun, S., Singer, H. J., Detman, T. R. and Kawano, H. (1998), \\"Magnetopause location under extreme solar wind conditions\\", J. Geophys. Res. 103(A8), 17691-17700, doi:10.1029/98JA01103 -- Table 1 \\"After Fit\\" row a1, p. 17,698: 10.22 +/- 0.10 R_E. The leading term of eq. 10.; Shue et al. (1998), doi:10.1029/98JA01103 -- Table 1 \\"After Fit\\" row a2, p. 17,698: 1.29 +/- 0.06 R_E. The amplitude of eq. 10\'s Bz term.; Shue et al. (1998), doi:10.1029/98JA01103 -- Table 1 \\"After Fit\\" row a5, p. 17,698: 6.6 +/- 0.5. The pressure exponent, r0 varying as Dp^(-1/a5).; Shue et al. (1998), doi:10.1029/98JA01103 -- Table 1 \\"After Fit\\" row a3, p. 17,698: 0.184 +/- 0.007 per nT. The scale inside eq. 10\'s hyperbolic tangent.; Shue et al. (1998), doi:10.1029/98JA01103 -- Table 1 \\"After Fit\\" row a4, p. 17,698: 8.14 +/- 0.39 nT. The Bz offset inside eq. 10\'s hyperbolic tangent.",\n            "source_note": "eq. 10 evaluated at the declared conditions (Bz = 0 nT, Dp = 2 nPa). Corroboration: Lugaz et al. (2016), doi:10.1038/ncomms13001 -- typical subsolar magnetopause 9-11 R_E, which contains this.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_MAGNETOPAUSE_STANDOFF_RADII",\n            "note": "Quiet-time nominal. Under storm compression it can fall inside geostationary orbit (6.6 R_E)."\n'),
